  "articleBody": "When you're upgrading your bathroom, choosing the right countertop material can feel like a big decision. Two popular choices are quartz and granite, and they both offer unique benefits and drawbacks. Quartz, for example, is engineered, meaning it's a non-porous material. This makes it super resistant to stains and etching, so you won't have to worry too much about spills or harsh cleaners. It also doesn't require sealing, which is a nice perk for busy homeowners. On the other hand, granite is a natural stone, and each slab has its own unique patterns and variations, giving your bathroom a truly one-of-a-kind look. However, because it's porous, granite does need to be sealed periodically to prevent staining and water absorption. When it comes to durability, both are pretty tough, but granite can be a bit more susceptible to chipping if something heavy is dropped on it. Quartz generally holds up well against everyday wear and tear. Considering things like maintenance, how you use your bathroom, and the aesthetic you're aiming for can help you decide. If you're looking for a low-maintenance, consistent look, quartz might be your go-to. But if you love the natural beauty and unique patterns of stone and don't mind a little extra upkeep, granite could be perfect. We can help you explore all the options and pick the best fit for your home; just give us a shout!",
